From ccaef23236f3a4aa201074c26587052472fda62d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diego <me@diegonmarcos.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 11:18:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] comms: fix 4 regressions found by independent review of patch
 0062
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adversarial multi-agent review of the just-shipped nav-drawer-tree patch
(4 reviewers x 3-vote verification) confirmed four real bugs, all fixed here:

1. Nav-drawer tree ordering: TupleAccountFolder.sort() only orders siblings
   by name — it has no concept of nesting, so a leaf could sort ahead of
   (or away from) its own parent group whenever the leaf's name alphabetized
   earlier (e.g. 'Logs/gitea' landing above an unrelated block, with the
   'Logs' header appearing later, disconnected from its child). Replaced the
   flat tree-annotation loop in AdapterNavAccountFolder.set() with a real
   depth-first re-flatten (flattenFolderTree): parent immediately followed
   by its children, sibling order preserved from the existing sort.

2. createRssFeed() (manual 'Add feed' dialog, the GitHub-releases auto-seed,
   and the Cloud-Feeds per-profile picker) never set notify/navigation.
   0062 deleted the old repairRssAccount loop that used to compensate for
   this on every app start — so those three provisioning paths regressed to
   permanently-invisible, never-notifying feed folders. Fixed by setting
   notify=true/navigation=true at creation, matching ensureLeafFolder's
   existing parity comment — same defaults-at-creation-not-forced-later
   principle 0062 already applied to the channel-tree path.

3. pruneFeedTree's orphan-group cleanup required f.parent != null, so a
   root-level (top-of-path) group folder could never be pruned once its
   last channel disappeared from channels.json — a permanent, empty, now
   navigation=true phantom folder in the drawer (0062 made groups visible;
   this bug made an already-existing dangling case visible too). Root
   groups now qualify for pruning like any other empty group.

4. The comms_nav_tree_0062 one-shot migration flag was written to
   SharedPreferences (non-transactional) before the channel-provisioning
   loop that follows it in the same method — so an exception partway
   through (e.g. a later channel insert) rolled back the migration's DB
   writes inside the caller's SQL transaction while the flag stayed
   permanently true, silently skipping the migration forever on retry.
   Moved the flag write to the end of ensureFeedTree, after everything
   (including pruneFeedTree) has succeeded.

Compile-verified manually (types/braces/imports checked by hand — this
machine is memory-exhausted right now under ~10 concurrent sessions and
gradle was OOM-killed on four consecutive attempts); relying on CI's
GitHub-hosted runner as the actual compile gate, per usual.

